  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 AM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nExpect more dry weather today. A weak low pressure \nsystem will move into the Pacific Northwest on Monday and through \nthe Idaho Panhandle Tuesday. This will bring rain and high elevation \nsnow to the area late Monday, through Monday night and into Tuesday. \nSnow will initially fall above 8000 feet and then fall to 6500 feet \nMonday night. QPF with this system would range from up to 0.10 \ninches for the Snake River Plain and Magic Valley to up to 0.25 \ninches for the mountains and highlands. The official WPC forecast is \naround or just below the 50th percentile. There's less than a 20% \nchance that most of the area will be dry with just a few hundredths \nfor the mountains and highlands and less than a 20% chance that this \ncould be a wetter than expected system with up to 0.25 inches for \nthe Snake River Plain and Magic Valley with 0.50 inches for the \nmountains and highlands. This is not going to be big snow maker \ncentral and east Idaho. The 50th percentile is for an inch or two \nabove 6500 feet and the 90th percentile only tacks on an additional \ninch. Winds will increase during the day on Monday and remain gusty \nMonday night. Gusts of 20 to 30 mph are likely Monday afternoon. \nGusts to 40 mph are likely Monday night, mainly across the Snake \nRiver Plain. Chances of reaching wind advisory criteria are less \nthan 30%. \n\nThis system lifts away from the area Wednesday. A stray shower may \nbe possible Wednesday. The models are showing another low pressure \nsystem moving into the Pacific Northwest late week which could \nresult in more precipitation across the area. Temperatures are like \na kiddie ride roller coaster. They will remain seasonable to start \nthe week, cool off slightly on Tuesday, and then return to seasonable \nlevels for mid week, but potentially falling back to slightly below \nnormal for late week.",
